          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why does low iron make people tired?",
          "answer": "The guidance I could search does not explain the biological mechanism behind tiredness from low iron. The passages I found describe the symptoms but not why they happen.\n\nThe **Iron-deficiency anemia** summary lists fatigue, reduced exercise tolerance, breathlessness on exertion, palpitations, headache, dizziness, pale skin, and cold hands and feet as common features. It also says the body often compensates well, so people may attribute months of growing tiredness to other causes before symptoms become noticeable.\n\nThe same summary notes that a blood count often finds the problem by chance, and that ferritin (a test of iron stores) can look normal during inflammation even when iron is low. Other iron studies can clarify this.\n\nThe search did not cover why iron deficiency causes fatigue at a cellular level. A clinician or a more detailed medical reference would be the best place to learn that.\n\nIf you are feeling tired and wondering whether iron is involved, a clinician can check a blood count and iron studies. If you notice black or tarry stools, or blood in your stool, contact a clinician promptly. The **Gastrointestinal bleeding** guidance notes that bleeding from higher in the gut can cause black stools and is a possible cause of iron deficiency that needs assessment.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
